# The layers whose remainders must move together, derived. One row per ordered pair reachable from
# each other through parts, plus the reflexive pair for each member.
#
# The fourth falsifier, computed
#   `F⁺ ∩ (F⁺)ᵀ`. `pm:Layer` states the falsifier: *if two candidate layers' remainders always move
#   together, they were never two layers.* If A is composed from B while B is composed from A, no
#   perturbation of one leaves the other fixed, so the pair fails the definition of a layer: the
#   two cells are one cell.
#
# The same defect in the other algebra: a rank deficiency
#   Composition over the layers is `x = F Phi x - e`, with `e` the filed eliminations, which asks
#   `(I - F Phi) x = -e` to have one solution. Two composed layers on a loop make `I - F Phi`
#   singular wherever the factors round the loop multiply to 1, which is what a loop of one unit
#   has: ordinal rank fails to exist and matrix rank falls at the same moment, for the same reason.
#   rank/evaluation_order.sqlc is the ordinal half.
#
# One self-join, not a second walk
#   composition/descent.sqlc is already the transitive closure, so intersecting it with its converse
#   gives an equivalence. The classes fall out of a window, because transitivity guarantees every
#   member sees the same set.
#
# Why the window sits outside the subquery
#   The deduplication happens in the subquery and the window runs over its result. A window beside
#   `SELECT DISTINCT` in one statement runs first, so `members` would count descent rows rather
#   than layers.
#
# Reading an empty result
#   Empty is the good outcome, and it is also what an unreached relation returns. It is empty on
#   this corpus.
#
# It does not replace the filer's attestation
#   `pm:Stack` requires `couplings` because nobody could check independence from the data, and it
#   still does. This relation lets the corpus contradict the filer's answer, which is strictly more
#   than it could do before and strictly less than deciding the answer.
#
# Probe
#   Close a loop across two documents inside a transaction and roll back; both this relation and
#   checks/layers_move_together must move.
-- composition/descent.sqlc intersected with its own converse; the classes of F+ ∩ (F+)ᵀ.
SELECT p.filing, p.layer, p.co_moves_with_filing, p.co_moves_with_layer,
       min(p.co_moves_with_filing || '/' || p.co_moves_with_layer) OVER w AS class,
       count(*) OVER w                                                    AS members
FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT a.root_filing AS filing, a.root_layer AS layer,
           a.filing AS co_moves_with_filing, a.layer AS co_moves_with_layer
    FROM      (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) a
    JOIN      (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) b ON  b.root_filing = a.filing      AND b.root_layer = a.layer
        AND b.filing      = a.root_filing AND b.layer      = a.root_layer
) p
WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY p.filing, p.layer)
